---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = "Trademark Class Guide (Classes 1–45) | BRNDNUR Advisory LLP";
const description = "Understand the Nice Classification system for Indian trademarks. Learn how to identify goods and services classes and avoid misclassification mistakes.";
const canonical = "https://brndnur.com/resources/trademark-class-guide/";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  currentPath="/resources/trademark-class-guide/"
>
  <main id="main-content">
    <!-- Breadcrumb & Hero -->
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="container">
        <nav class="bn-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <a href="/">Home</a>
          <span class="sep">/</span>
          <a href="/resources/">Resources</a>
          <span class="sep">/</span>
          <span class="current">Trademark Class Guide</span>
        </nav>
        <p class="eyebrow">Classification Guide</p>
        <h1>Trademark Class Guide: The NICE Classification Explained.</h1>
        <p class="lead">How Indian trademarks are classified across 45 categories of goods and services, and how to select the right scope for your brand.</p>
        
        <div class="hero-badges">
          <div class="hero-badge-item">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><rect x="3" y="3" width="7" height="7"/><rect x="14" y="3" width="7" height="7"/><rect x="14" y="14" width="7" height="7"/><rect x="3" y="14" width="7" height="7"/></svg>
            <span>45 International NICE Classes</span>
          </div>
          <div class="hero-badge-item">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 2 3 6v14a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h14a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V6l-3-4z"/></svg>
            <span>Classes 1–34: Physical Goods</span>
          </div>
          <div class="hero-badge-item">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M16 21v-2a4 4 0 0 0-4-4H6a4 4 0 0 0-4 4v2"/><circle cx="9" cy="7" r="4"/></svg>
            <span>Classes 35–45: Commercial Services</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="actions" style="margin-top: 28px;">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/trademark-classes/">View Commercial Classes &rarr;</a>
          <a class="btn btn-outline" href="/resources/">Back to Resources</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Guide Content -->
    <section class="section">
      <div class="container">
        <article class="legal-prose" style="max-width: 860px; margin: 0 auto;">
          <div class="advisory-notice" style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 32px;">
            <div class="advisory-notice-icon">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12.01" y2="16"/></svg>
            </div>
            <div class="advisory-notice-content">
              <h4>Classification Core Rule</h4>
              <p>Under Indian trademark law, registration is strictly class-specific. Owning a trademark in Class 25 (Apparel) does not prevent an unrelated entity from registering the same name in Class 30 (Spices) unless your mark is officially declared a Well-Known mark under Section 11(8).</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h2>1. The Architecture of the Nice Classification</h2>
          <p>India follows the Nice Agreement concerning the International Classification of Goods and Services. The system divides commerce into 45 classes:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Classes 1 to 34 (Goods):</strong> Physical products ranging from raw agricultural produce and industrial chemicals to packaged foods, textiles, electronics, and cosmetics.</li>
            <li><strong>Classes 35 to 45 (Services):</strong> Commercial activities including advertising and retail (Class 35), financial operations (Class 36), telecommunications (Class 38), software & SaaS (Class 42), and hospitality & dining (Class 43).</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>2. Most Common Entrepreneurial Classes in India</h2>
          <div class="bn-table-wrap">
            <table class="bn-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Class</th>
                  <th>Domain</th>
                  <th>Typical Covered Offerings</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Class 25</strong></td>
                  <td>Clothing & Fashion</td>
                  <td>Ready-made garments, footwear, headgear, sportswear</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Class 29</strong></td>
                  <td>Food & Dairy</td>
                  <td>Oils, milk, preserved fruits, meat, edible fats</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Class 30</strong></td>
                  <td>Spices & Bakery</td>
                  <td>Spices, tea, coffee, bakery goods, confectionery, snacks</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Class 35</strong></td>
                  <td>Retail & E-Com</td>
                  <td>Retail stores, supermarkets, e-commerce platforms, marketing</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Class 42</strong></td>
                  <td>Tech & SaaS</td>
                  <td>Software engineering, cloud hosting, SaaS platforms, IT design</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Class 43</strong></td>
                  <td>Restaurants</td>
                  <td>Restaurants, cafes, cloud kitchens, catering, hotel stays</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h2>3. Multi-Class Applications (Form TM-A)</h2>
          <p>Under the Trade Marks Rules, 2017, applicants can file a single application covering multiple classes. However, statutory government fees multiply with each added class (₹4,500/class for MSMEs/individuals, ₹9,000/class for standard companies).</p>

          <h2>4. Cross-Class Conflicts</h2>
          <p>Registry examiners inspect closely allied classes. For example, a restaurant brand in Class 43 that expands into selling packaged curry powders in Class 30 could encounter relative grounds of objection under Section 11 if a confusingly similar brand already occupies Class 30.</p>
        </article>

        <div class="cta-panel" style="margin-top: 48px;">
          <p class="eyebrow">Need Class Guidance?</p>
          <h2>Map Your Commercial Catalog with Our Advisors.</h2>
          <p>Send us your product list for a tailored classification assessment.</p>
          <div class="cta-actions">
            <a class="btn btn-gold" href="https://wa.me/919048188899?text=Hello%20BRNDNUR%2C%20please%20help%20me%20map%20my%20trademark%20classes." target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Inquire on WhatsApp &rarr;</a>
            <a class="btn btn-outline" href="/trademark-classes/">View Classes Page</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
